-- title:
--   Theorem 1.2 — randomness is necessary for adaptive data analysis
-- statement:
--   There exist absolute constants $C_X,C_k>0$ such that for every integer $n>2$ there are a finite domain $X$ and a positive horizon $k$, both fixed before the mechanism, satisfying
--   $$|X|\le2^{C_Xn^2\log_2 n},\qquad k\le C_kn\log_2n.$$
--   For every deterministic mechanism $m$ on $X$ with sample size $n$, there exist a fixed distribution $P$ uniform on exactly $20n$ distinct points and a deterministic adaptive Boolean-query analyst $A$ such that
--   $$\Pr_{S\sim P^n}\left[\exists j\le k:\ |a_j-\mathbb E_{x\sim P}q_j(x)|>\frac1{10}\right]\ge\frac9{10}.$$
--   The probability uses the actual interaction law generated by independent ordered sampling with replacement. The mechanism receives the full query function and may return any real answer; it has no distribution input. The analyst may depend on the mechanism and $P$ but sees the sample only through answers. Neither computation nor query-description length is bounded. This is the full plain-model randomness lower bound, including one mechanism-independent domain and the fixed-support, high-probability attack.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: an existential finite domain X and a distribution uniform on 20n points → X=Fin d and the pushforward of the uniform law along an injection Fin(20n)→Fin d, with explicit positive d and k; reason: every finite domain is equivalent to Fin d, and injection makes the exact distinct support and its equal masses explicit; positivity is implied by the source setting
--   - encoding: domain size 2^{O(n² log n)} and O(n log n) queries → positive absolute real constants before n, an explicit integer horizon before the mechanism, and base-two logarithms; reason: this resolves the source asymptotics uniformly for all n>2; changing log base changes only the existential constants
--   - generalization: an analyst whose private randomness may be included in the success probability → a deterministic adaptive Boolean-query analyst with the same success bound over the iid sample alone; reason: the finite experiment on pp.12–14 has success at least 199/200; fixing the locations and then the analyst coins by averaging yields a deterministic witness before sampling
-- source:
--   Edith Cohen, Haim Kaplan, Yishay Mansour, Shay Sapir, Uri Stemmer, Eliad Tsfadia, STOC 2027 submission #267 (October 2026), Theorem 1.2 and Remark 1.3, p.3; full proof §§4.2–4.4, pp.10–14; https://github.com/sigact-stoc/stoc2027/releases/download/papers-1/267.pdf; PDF/printed pages coincide.

import Definitions.Def_IsRandomnessNecessaryADA_Plain_Protocol

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace IsRandomnessNecessaryADA.Plain

/-- Theorem 1.2, p.3, full strength. Absolute constants, domain and horizon precede
all mechanisms. The target distribution is fixed before the iid sample is drawn. -/
theorem theorem_1_2 :
    ∃ C_X C_k : ℝ, 0 < C_X ∧ 0 < C_k ∧
      ∀ (n : ℕ) (hn : 2 < n), ∃ d k : ℕ,
        0 < d ∧ 0 < k ∧
        (d : ℝ) ≤ (2 : ℝ) ^ (C_X * (n : ℝ) ^ 2 * (Real.log n / Real.log 2)) ∧
        (k : ℝ) ≤ C_k * n * (Real.log n / Real.log 2) ∧
        ∀ m : Mechanism (Fin d) n,
          ∃ (e : Fin (20 * n) ↪ Fin d) (A : BooleanAnalyst (Fin d)),
            (9 / 10 : ℝ) ≤
              probability (interactionLaw m (liftAnalyst A) (uniformSupport (by omega) e) k)
                (errorEvent (uniformSupport (by omega) e) (1 / 10)) := by sorry

end IsRandomnessNecessaryADA.Plain
